The main objective of this paper is to investigate multiple solution possibilities for a specific Kirchhoff type elliptic system of equations that are given by the system of equations \begin{equation*}
\begin{cases}
\left( \int_{\Omega} k(\omega(x))\,dx\right) \left( \sum_{i=1}^m \frac{\partial u_i}{\partial x_i} \right)=\sum_{i=1}^m \frac{\partial p_i}{\partial x_i} =\sum_{i=1}^m \frac{\partial f_i(x,u_1(x),\ldots,u_m(x))}{\partial x_i}, \quad x\in\Omega, \\
u=0, \quad x\in\partial\Omega,
\end{cases}
\end{equation*}where $\Omega$ is a bounded domain within the $n$-dimensional space, and the function $f$ is able to be changed depending on $x$, on some variables $u_1(x),\ldots,u_m(x)$, and it meets certain requirements. We are also able to prove that we have multiple solution possibilities if the function $f$ is changed depending on $u_i(x)$ for $i=1,2,\ldots,m$. We use the variational method to solve this problem, and we must establish the compactness of the embedding associated
